he enum's binary codes, 00, 01 and 10, fit in two bits, and Vivado kept them. With its usual settings it chose one-hot, one flip-flop for each of the three states. Here the logic around the two flip-flops takes four small LUTs.</p>","type":"report","source":"vivado/reports/door2_keep/arty-a7-100t/utilization.rpt","firstLine":180,"excerpt":["+----------+------+---------------------+","| Ref Name | Used | Functional Category |","+----------+------+---------------------+","| IBUF     |    4 |                  IO |","| OBUF     |    2 |                  IO |","| LUT2     |    2 |                 LUT |","| FDRE     |    2 |        Flop & Latch |","| LUT5     |    1 |                 LUT |","| LUT4     |    1 |                 LUT |","| BUFG     |    1 |               Clock |","+----------+------+---------------------+"],"ask":"How many FDRE flip-flops did Vivado use for door2_keep?","answer":"2","numeric":true},{"id":"enc-fill-range","itemId":"state-encoding","role":"core","promptHtml":"A controller has three states, <code class=\"nobreak\">IDLE</code>, <code class=\"nobreak\">BUSY</code> and <code class=\"nobreak\">DONE</code>, in a binary encoding. Type the range that gives each state a code of its own with the fewest bits.","explanationHtml":"<p>Three codes need two bits: 00, 01 and 10, with 11 left over. One bit holds only two codes, and three bits would waste one; one-hot, with a bit for each state, would need three.</p>","type":"fill","file":"hdl/level-05/mul_repeat/mul_repeat.sv","firstLine":16,"totalLines":50,"before":[],"blankLine":{"left":"  typedef enum logic ","right":" {IDLE, BUSY, DONE} state_t;"},"after":["  state_t     state, next;"],"highlighted":{"before":[],"blankLine":{"left":[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"typedef","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"enum","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--co